Invariant: after round j, `dist[v]` is the minimum cost of any walk from s to v using at most j edges in the relevant subgraph, or None if none exists. Copying the previous distances preserves shorter walks, and relaxing each edge extends exactly the walks allowed by the previous round. This proves the invariant by induction and also justifies the early exit: a round with no change is a fixed point.

Every vertex on an s-to-t walk is both reachable from s and able to reach t, so filtering loses no candidate walk. If the relevant subgraph has no negative cycle, removing cycles from a walk cannot increase its cost; consequently a minimum-cost simple path exists and uses at most k−1 edges. If another relaxation improves a distance, its witnessing walk has at most k edges and beats all walks with at most k−1 edges. It must contain a negative cycle, since otherwise removing a repeated-vertex cycle would produce a shorter walk of no greater cost, contradicting that improvement. That cycle is reachable from s and can reach t, so repeating it gives arbitrarily negative s-to-t costs. Conversely, any such negative cycle rules out a fixed point: summing the inequalities `dist[v] <= dist[u] + weight` around it would contradict its negative total weight.

With m edges, the worst-case time is O(n(n+m)), including distance-array copies each round, and auxiliary memory is O(n+m). Under the supplied bounds, every computed finite candidate is a sum of at most n edge weights, so its magnitude is at most 2×10^15; integer operations therefore have bounded size here. Parallel edges and self-loops require no special handling. When s equals t, the empty walk has cost zero, unless a reachable negative cycle can also return to s, in which case the result is UNBOUNDED.
